cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "USearch: Billion-Scale Multi-Modal Search, Clustering, & Sparse Algebra Engine for CPUs, GPUs, & NVMe Devices"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
license: Apache-2.0
version: 3.0.0-rc.1
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.7949415
repository-code: https://github.com/unum-cloud/USearch
url: https://github.com/unum-cloud/USearch
abstract: >-
  USearch is a similarity-search engine generalizable to sparse linear algebra and machine-learning
  workloads, designed to put each part of the machine on the work it is best at: GPUs for the
  compute- and bandwidth-bound passes, CPUs for the latency-bound ones, and NVMe solid-state drives
  for scale beyond what memory holds. It indexes dense embeddings, biological and textual sequences,
  geospatial coordinates, and molecular geometries, each addressed as its own modality over a shared
  proximity graph rather than flattened into a single embedding pipeline. That same graph serves as
  a sparse operand, folding matrix products, late-interaction MaxSim scoring, and softmax attention
  into the traversal rather than materializing a match list. It carries native support for x86, Arm,
  RISC-V, and WebAssembly instruction sets, spanning AVX2 through AVX-512, AVX10.2 and Intel AMX,
  NEON through SVE2 and SME, RVV, and relaxed SIMD, with the arm chosen from what the host reports
  at run time. Rows reside in host memory, device memory, or a block-addressable array reached by
  asynchronous I/O, and one checkpoint materializes into any of the three without being rewritten.
  Quantization spans Float64 down to NVFP4, packed nibbles and single bits, trading precision for
  footprint per row, across Linux, Windows, macOS and beyond. Native bindings cover C, C++, Python,
  Rust, Go, Java, JavaScript, Swift, C#, Zig, Mojo, and Wolfram, with Kotlin and Scala consuming the
  Java surface directly.
keywords:
  - vector-search
  - similarity-search
  - nearest-neighbors
  - clustering
  - multi-modal
  - quantization
  - simd
  - gpu

